[$in title:["Language basics"]]
[$in description:["Blocks, comments, naming, core values, strings and constructor syntax in Moth."]]
[$in head:[language_theme_head]]

[navbar]
[sidebar]
listed = sync_navigation(sidebar_id)!
[section, $md:
    # Language basics

    Moth aims to make code easy to reason about locally. You can often answer a question by reading a declaration and the expression that uses it. Start with a window size. What does it store, and what does the caller need to supply?

    ## Declare fields and supply arguments

    [codeblock, $code "moth":
        Size = | width I32, height I32 = 720 |

        screen = Size(1280)
        wide = Size(height = 900, width = 1440)
    ]

    `Size` declares a struct. The names between pipes describe its fields. The constructor takes arguments in parentheses. In the first call, `1280` fills the first field and the declared default supplies the height. The second call names both fields, so you can read which dimension each number sets without counting positions.

    The outer `=` binds a name to the completed value. Inside `Size(...)`, the label in a named argument identifies a field. For example, `height = 900` supplies a value to the constructor without creating a variable called `height` in the caller.

    ## Pass a value to a function

    [codeblock, $code "moth":
        describe |size Size| -> String {
            return [:[size.width] x [size.height]]
        }

        caption = describe(screen)
    ]

    Pipes after a function name declare its parameters. Here `size` names the argument inside `describe`. The fields `width` and `height` belong to the value itself. The arrow gives the return type and braces enclose the statements. The template inserts the two dimensions into text, so `caption` holds `"1280 x 720"`.

    ## Read brackets with their receiving type

    [codeblock, $code "moth":
        title = ["Moth", " Guide"]
        parts [String] = ["Moth", " Guide"]
        sizes [String = Size] = ["screen" = screen]
    ]

    The first two expressions contain identical text. With no other type to guide it, the all-String head joins the parts into `"Moth Guide"`. The type on `parts` asks for a collection, so it keeps two separate Strings, including the space at the start of the second one. In the third expression, the keyed entry places `screen` in a map. Passing `title` to another function later cannot turn it into a collection.

    ## Follow a name through a block

    [codeblock, $code "moth":
        State = { Waiting, Ready | size Size | }

        label |state State| -> String {
            return if state is {
                case Waiting: then "waiting"
                case Ready(size): then describe(size)
            }
        }

        message = label(State::Ready(screen))
    ]

    `State` declares two constructors. Only `Ready` has a field. At the call site, `::` selects that constructor and the parentheses supply its field. In a pattern, `Ready(size)` binds the stored field to the name `size` for that arm.

    Braces mark a block's boundaries. The construct decides the scope of each name. Both arms belong to the same match block, but only the `Ready` arm can use its capture. `then` supplies the chosen arm's result and `return` sends it back to the caller.

    You will see the same building blocks in other places. A loop uses pipes to name the item for one iteration. A generic parameter lets a declaration work with several types. A named region selects a storage lifetime and a directive such as `$md` selects formatting for a text construction. The @../cheatsheet (cheatsheet) collects the forms, and the @../progress (progress matrix) tracks compiler support.
]

